<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Archbishopric of Riga</h1><p class="st-meta">1186 CE – 1561 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ecclesiastical principality that led the Christian conquest of Livonia and governed much of Latvia for nearly four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-meinhard">Meinhard</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livs</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikskile">Üxküll</a> in 1186. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Buxhoeveden</a> founded Riga in 1201 and moved the see there, directing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>. In 1255 Riga was raised to an archbishopric under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-suerbeer">Albert Suerbeer</a>, with authority over the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The archbishops were princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, ruling lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauja">Gauja</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and castles such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turaida">Turaida</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-koknese">Kokenhusen</a>. Their rule was marked by long conflicts with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> and with the citizens of Riga over control of the city. The cathedral chapter and vassals sat in the Livonian diets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> reached Riga in the 1520s, weakening the archbishops. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> the last archbishop, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-of-brandenburg">Wilhelm of Brandenburg</a>, placed his lands under Polish-Lithuanian protection, and in 1561-1562 they were secularised and incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-livonia">Duchy of Livonia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/archbishopric-of-riga.jpg" alt="Archbishopric of Riga" width="320" height="347"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Termer assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Livonia_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1186 CE – 1561 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meinhard, Albert of Riga, Wilhelm of Brandenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Middle Low German, Latvian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Latvia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Archbishopric of Riga, Erzstift Riga</dd></dl></aside></div>
